> 1. Virtualization & Hotplugging: In QEMU-emulated environments or
> cloud infrastructures, virtual PCI devices can be dynamically
> attached. An untrusted guest or a compromised emulation layer could
> trigger this exception. As shown in the detailed crash log [1], this
> unhandled exception results in a KASAN slab-out-of-bounds during the
> module init path, leading to a soft lockup of the host/guest.

Does anybody actually care? I don't trust a VM if i don't trust the
host of the VM. In order to do this sort of attack, you need to
compromise the VM host, which already means it is game over.

> 2. Fuzzing Roadblocks: This divide-by-zero is a frequent "roadblock"
> for automated fuzzers like syzkaller.

Make a better fuzzer? If this happens a lot, why not teach it how to
avoid the problem?

But why would you want to fuz a device which nobody has any more?  We
are probably just going to delete the whole driver.

